> I'm a novice player (almost always die before level 5 novice) and I
> have a lot of problems managing my inventory at the beginning of the
> game.  I tend to get burdened because I'm carrying around a lot of
> stuff that I haven't had the chance to identify yet.  Two questions: 1)
> what items should I leave behind initially, and 2) what's a safe way to
> leave things behind so that other creatures won't take them?

I remember when I was at that stage. Don't give up on the game, cause
that was how I was just about 6 months ago and I just ascended my first
character a few days ago. Here are some tips for early survival..

Keep in good standing with your god. Use prayer when you're low on HP
at the beginning or if you're weak and have no food. Don't equip
unidentified armor (including those tempting iron skull caps). Make
sure it isn't cursed with an altar first. Keep this spoiler up as you
play <http://www.geocities.com/dcorbett42/nethack/corpse.htm> until
it's second nature to know every intrinsic you can get from a corpse
and which ones to never eat. The easiest characters to play are
Samurai, Valkyrie and Barbarian, and I would recommend ascending one of
these three before moving on. Others find wizards to be just as
powerful or even moreso, but I really just can't keep them alive
personally.

A pretty "safe" way early game is to find a crude dagger (or some other
crap edged weapon you'd never want) and engrave Elbereth, and leave the
items on the engraving. You will need to engrave "Elber" and then "eth"
or else the weapon will completely dull before you finish writing the
word. Peaceful monsters ignore Elbereth as do some others (see the
Elbereth spoiler for more info).

Heavy items to drop are mostly armor and spellbooks. There are a few
types of armor I never wear, namely all types of Plate Mail. They weigh
450 each, and even a strong character can only hold 900-1000 weight.
Banded mail is also heavy. Also don't carry a pick-axe until later,
it's not very useful early game anyway. Don't carry more than one or
two backup weapons either, especially weapons like polearms or battle
axes. Those weigh between 100-180.  Spellbooks weigh 50 each.

Scrolls weigh 5 each and potions weigh 20 each. If you find 10 potions
early on it adds up pretty quick. Drinking unidentified potions is
dangerous, but sometimes I'll drink or throw a potion to identify it if
I have multiples of that potion. When doing this you should stand on an
engraving of Elbereth in case it's a potion of paralysis, sleeping,
confusion, etc.

The only other really heavy items are crystal balls (150 weight)  and
tinning kits (100). If I find myself burdened and have one of these
items they are usually among the first items to drop. Monsters can't
use them against you so they'll always be there when you want to go
back and get them.

>> You'll often find chests and boxes that are a safe place where monsters
>> won't look into. You may kick open chests that are locked (though that
>> can destroy glass items that have been in the container).

>You can also use a lockpick, or try #forcing the lock.

Messing with chests early game is a bad idea in my opinion. When I
first started I just HAD to get into those chests. But the fact of the
matter is, before you have a method for unlocking them (credit card,
lock pick or key) chests can be pretty dangerous. First of all they are
often trapped. If you are level 1-3 it's not rare at all for a trap to
be fatal. Second of all kicking a locked chest or #forcing with a blunt
weapon can break fragile objects, or just destroy the chest breaking
everything in it. Finally, #forcing can break your weapon, and at low
levels losing your starting weapon can spell game over for you.

My policy is I never touch a chest until I have a method for unlocking
or I have both over 40 HP and poison resistance. (The chest was
trapped! A needle shoots at you! The needle was poisoned! The poison
was deadly! DYWYPI?) Mine town has a pretty good chance of having a key
or lock pick in the tool shop, and those items aren't very uncommon
anyway. 

Good luck and hope this helps!



